            <ArticleTitle>Transcutaneous nerve stimulation for the treatment of postoperative pain.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>253-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Postoperative pain and rehabilitation parameters were compared in groups of patients with or without transcutaneous electric nerve stimulation (TENS) after orthopedic surgery. There was a subjective improvement, but only a non significant difference in opiate requirements with the use of TENS postoperatively; rehabilitation parameters turned out to be of limited value in evaluating its effect. Two patients had an allergic reaction after the use of TENS for postoperative pain relief.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
